[{"category":"section","location":"observation/#Observation-models","page":"Observation models","text":"```@docs bayesian_model","title":"Observation models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BayesianDiffEq.EulerMaruyamaPseudoLikelihood","page":"API reference","text":"EulerMaruyamaPseudoLikelihood(f, g, t, observed)\n\nEuler-Maruyama pseudo-likelihood of the parameters theta of an Ito SDE\n\ndX(t) = f(X(t), theta) dt + g(X(t), theta) dW(t)\n\ngiven discrete observations of a subset of the state at times t.\n\nArguments\n\nf::Function: drift, f(x, theta) -> Vector (same length as x).\ng::Function: diffusion coefficient, g(x, theta) -> Vector.\nt: strictly increasing vector of observation times.\nobserved::Vector{Int}: indices of the observed states (the remaining states are treated as latent and imputed by the pseudo-likelihood).\n\nExample\n\nem = EulerMaruyamaPseudoLikelihood(f, g, t, [1])\nll = loglikelihood_em(em, theta, data)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.EulerMaruyamaPseudoLikelihood"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BayesianDiffEq.ObservationSpec","page":"API reference","text":"ObservationSpec\n\nSpecification of how the underlying dynamical state is observed.\n\nFields\n\nlikelihood::Symbol: :gaussian (continuous, homoscedastic) or :poisson (count data).\nobserved::Vector{Int}: indices of the state vector that are observed. Empty means all states.\nscale::Float64: exposure/scaling for the Poisson mean (rate = scale * state).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.ObservationSpec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BayesianDiffEq.bayesian_model-Tuple{SciMLBase.AbstractDEProblem, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"bayesian_model(prob, t, data; theta_priors, likelihood = :gaussian,\n               sigma_prior = truncated(Normal(0, 1); lower = 1e-4),\n               u0_priors = nothing, scale = 1.0)\n\nConstruct a Turing DynamicPPL.Model that infers the parameters of the SciML problem prob from observations data at times t.\n\nArguments\n\nprob: an ODEProblem or SDEProblem (its parameters are placeholders and will be sampled).\nt: vector of observation times (the solver is run with saveat = t).\ndata: Vector{Vector} of the same length as t; data[j][i] is the observation of state observed[i] at time t[j].\ntheta_priors::Vector{Distribution}: prior for each parameter, in the order of prob.p.\nlikelihood::Symbol: :gaussian or :poisson.\nsigma_prior::Distribution: prior on the observation noise (Gaussian case only).\nu0_priors::Union{Nothing,Vector{Distribution}}: if given, the initial condition of each state is treated as an unknown quantity with the given prior.\nscale::Real: exposure for the Poisson likelihood.\n\nReturns\n\nA DynamicPPL.Model ready for sample or maximum_a_posteriori.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.bayesian_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BayesianDiffEq.compare_bayes_vs_optimization-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"compare_bayes_vs_optimization(chain, theta_hat, theta_true; alpha = 0.9)\n\nGiven posterior samples chain (a MCMCChains.Chains), an optimization-based point estimate theta_hat, and ground truth theta_true, return a NamedTuple with the posterior mean, the optimization estimate, the error of each, the posterior standard deviation, and whether the alpha credible interval covers the truth.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.compare_bayes_vs_optimization"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BayesianDiffEq.coverage_of_credible_intervals-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"coverage_of_credible_intervals(coverage_runs; alpha = 0.9)\n\nGiven a vector of NamedTuples or pairs (truth, draws) where draws is an n_params x n_samples matrix of posterior samples, compute the empirical coverage of the alpha central credible intervals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.coverage_of_credible_intervals"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BayesianDiffEq.hierarchical_model-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"hierarchical_model(prob, t, data_all; theta_priors, u0_priors = nothing,\n                   likelihood = :gaussian, sigma_prior = truncated(Normal(0, 1); lower = 1e-4),\n                   scale = 1.0, n_subjects = nothing)\n\nConstruct a hierarchical Turing model for a population of related dynamical systems. Each subject j has parameters theta_j = mu .+ tau .* z_j (non-centered), with mu ~ product_distribution(theta_priors) and a half-normal prior on the population scale tau.\n\nArguments\n\ndata_all::Vector{Vector{Vector}}: data_all[j] is the data vector of subject j, in the same format as bayesian_model.\nn_subjects: inferred from data_all if not given.\n\nReturns\n\nA DynamicPPL.Model whose parameters include mu, tau, per-subject theta_j and sigma_j, and (optionally) initial conditions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.hierarchical_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BayesianDiffEq.loglikelihood_em-Tuple{EulerMaruyamaPseudoLikelihood, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"loglikelihood_em(em::EulerMaruyamaPseudoLikelihood, theta, data, u0; dt_sub = nothing)\n\nEvaluate the Euler-Maruyama pseudo-log-likelihood of theta and u0 given observations data (a Vector{Vector} aligned with em.t).\n\nIf dt_sub is given, each inter-observation interval is sub-divided into steps of size at most dt_sub and the unobserved states are imputed by their Euler-Maruyama means; this stabilizes the likelihood when the observation interval is long relative to the dynamics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.loglikelihood_em"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BayesianDiffEq.map_initialization-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"map_initialization(model; maxiters = 2000, fallback = NamedTuple())\n\nCompute a maximum-a-posteriori estimate of model using L-BFGS and return it as a NamedTuple suitable for the init_params keyword of sample. Returns fallback (default: empty NamedTuple, i.e. no explicit initialization) if the optimization does not converge.\n\nExample\n\ninit = map_initialization(model)\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 500; init_params = init)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.map_initialization"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BayesianDiffEq.optimization_fit-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"optimization_fit(prob, t, data; theta0, lb = nothing, ub = nothing,\n                 solver = Tsit5(), observed = nothing)\n\nFit the parameters of prob to data by minimizing the squared error between the model solution and the observations. This is the optimization-based point estimate that pseudo-likelihood Bayesian methods should be compared against.\n\nReturns (theta_hat, retcode).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.optimization_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BayesianDiffEq.posterior_predictive-Tuple{Any, Any, AbstractMatrix}","page":"API reference","text":"posterior_predictive(prob, t, theta_draws; solver = Tsit5(), kwargs...)\n\nSimulate the dynamical system at each posterior draw. Returns a vector of solutions, one per column of theta_draws (size n_params x n_samples), which can be plotted against the data as a posterior predictive check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.posterior_predictive"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BayesianDiffEq.sbc-Tuple{Function, Int64}","page":"API reference","text":"sbc(model_generator::Function, n_rep::Int; sampler = NUTS(), n_samples = 500)\n\nSimulation-based calibration (Talts et al., 2018). For n_rep rounds: draw parameters from the prior, simulate data, sample the posterior, and record the rank of the true parameter within the posterior draws. For a correctly specified model and sampler the ranks are uniform; strong deviations (U-shapes or spikes) indicate bias or over/under-confidence.\n\nmodel_generator is a function of no arguments returning a (model, theta_true, data) triple.\n\nReturns a matrix of ranks, size (n_params, n_rep).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.sbc"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BayesianDiffEq.sde_brownian_expansion-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}, Int64}","page":"API reference","text":"sde_brownian_expansion(W_std, n_coeffs; tspan = (0.0, 1.0))\n\nTruncated Karhunen-Loeve / Wiener-Legendre expansion of a standard Brownian motion on tspan, evaluated at n_coeffs basis functions. Returns a function W(t) of time, so that SDEs driven by this W can be integrated as random ODEs. This is the building block of the \"SDE as ODE\" construction used to compare pseudo-likelihood to optimization-based SDE fitting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.sde_brownian_expansion"},{"category":"section","location":"hierarchical/#Hierarchical-models","page":"Hierarchical models","text":"","title":"Hierarchical models"},{"category":"function","location":"hierarchical/#BayesianDiffEq.hierarchical_model","page":"Hierarchical models","text":"hierarchical_model(prob, t, data_all; theta_priors, u0_priors = nothing,\n                   likelihood = :gaussian, sigma_prior = truncated(Normal(0, 1); lower = 1e-4),\n                   scale = 1.0, n_subjects = nothing)\n\nConstruct a hierarchical Turing model for a population of related dynamical systems. Each subject j has parameters theta_j = mu .+ tau .* z_j (non-centered), with mu ~ product_distribution(theta_priors) and a half-normal prior on the population scale tau.\n\nArguments\n\ndata_all::Vector{Vector{Vector}}: data_all[j] is the data vector of subject j, in the same format as bayesian_model.\nn_subjects: inferred from data_all if not given.\n\nReturns\n\nA DynamicPPL.Model whose parameters include mu, tau, per-subject theta_j and sigma_j, and (optionally) initial conditions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.hierarchical_model"},{"category":"section","location":"sde/#SDE-pseudo-likelihood","page":"SDE pseudo-likelihood","text":"```@docs loglikelihoodem sdebrownianexpansion optimizationfit comparebayesvs_optimization","title":"SDE pseudo-likelihood"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Posterior-validation","page":"Validation","text":"","title":"Posterior validation"},{"category":"function","location":"validation/#BayesianDiffEq.sbc","page":"Validation","text":"sbc(model_generator::Function, n_rep::Int; sampler = NUTS(), n_samples = 500)\n\nSimulation-based calibration (Talts et al., 2018). For n_rep rounds: draw parameters from the prior, simulate data, sample the posterior, and record the rank of the true parameter within the posterior draws. For a correctly specified model and sampler the ranks are uniform; strong deviations (U-shapes or spikes) indicate bias or over/under-confidence.\n\nmodel_generator is a function of no arguments returning a (model, theta_true, data) triple.\n\nReturns a matrix of ranks, size (n_params, n_rep).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.sbc"},{"category":"function","location":"validation/#BayesianDiffEq.coverage_of_credible_intervals","page":"Validation","text":"coverage_of_credible_intervals(coverage_runs; alpha = 0.9)\n\nGiven a vector of NamedTuples or pairs (truth, draws) where draws is an n_params x n_samples matrix of posterior samples, compute the empirical coverage of the alpha central credible intervals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.coverage_of_credible_intervals"},{"category":"function","location":"validation/#BayesianDiffEq.posterior_predictive","page":"Validation","text":"posterior_predictive(prob, t, theta_draws; solver = Tsit5(), kwargs...)\n\nSimulate the dynamical system at each posterior draw. Returns a vector of solutions, one per column of theta_draws (size n_params x n_samples), which can be plotted against the data as a posterior predictive check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.posterior_predictive"},{"category":"function","location":"validation/#BayesianDiffEq.map_initialization","page":"Validation","text":"map_initialization(model; maxiters = 2000, fallback = NamedTuple())\n\nCompute a maximum-a-posteriori estimate of model using L-BFGS and return it as a NamedTuple suitable for the init_params keyword of sample. Returns fallback (default: empty NamedTuple, i.e. no explicit initialization) if the optimization does not converge.\n\nExample\n\ninit = map_initialization(model)\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 500; init_params = init)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianDiffEq.map_initialization"},{"category":"section","location":"#BayesianDiffEq.jl","page":"Home","text":"Modern Bayesian parameter estimation for DifferentialEquations.jl models, built on Turing.jl.\n\nusing BayesianDiffEq\n\nmodel = bayesian_model(prob, t, data;\n                       theta_priors = priors,\n                       likelihood = :poisson)\ninit = map_initialization(model)\nchain = sample(model, NUTS(), 500; init_params = init)\n\nPages = [\"observation.md\", \"sde.md\", \"hierarchical.md\", \"validation.md\", \"api.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"BayesianDiffEq.jl"}]
